# Phoenix + Elixir Guidelines

This project is a web application written with the Phoenix web framework.

## Project guidelines

- Use `mix precommit` when you are done with all changes and fix any pending issues.
- Use `:req` (`Req`) for HTTP requests, and avoid `:httpoison`, `:tesla`, and `:httpc`.

## Phoenix v1.8 guidelines

- Always begin LiveView templates with `<Layouts.app flash={@flash} ...>` and wrap all inner content with it.
- `MyAppWeb.Layouts` is aliased in `my_app_web.ex`, so you can use it without aliasing again.
- If you see `current_scope` assign errors, you missed Authenticated Routes or did not pass `current_scope` to `<Layouts.app>`; fix by moving routes to the proper `live_session` and ensuring `current_scope` is passed.
- `<.flash_group>` moved to the `Layouts` module; never call `<.flash_group>` outside `layouts.ex`.
- Use `<.icon>` for hero icons, and never use `Heroicons` modules or similar.
- Use the imported `<.input>` component for form inputs from `core_components.ex` when available.
- If you override `<.input class="...">`, no default classes are inherited, so your classes must fully style the input.

## JS and CSS guidelines

- Use Tailwind CSS classes and custom CSS rules to create polished, responsive, visually stunning interfaces.
- Tailwindcss v4 does not need `tailwind.config.js` and uses this import syntax in `app.css`:

```js
@import "tailwindcss" source(none);
@source "../css";
@source "../js";
@source "../../lib/my_app_web";
```

- Always maintain this import syntax in `app.css` for projects generated with `phx.new`.
- Never use `@apply` when writing raw CSS.
- Always manually write Tailwind-based components instead of using daisyUI for a unique, world-class design.
- Only the `app.js` and `app.css` bundles are supported.
- Do not reference external vendor script `src` or link `href` in layouts.
- Import vendor deps into `app.js` and `app.css` when needed.
- Never write inline `<script>custom js</script>` tags within templates.

## UI/UX and design guidelines

- Produce world-class UI designs with a focus on usability, aesthetics, and modern design principles.
- Implement subtle micro-interactions like button hover effects and smooth transitions.
- Ensure clean typography, spacing, and layout balance for a refined, premium look.
- Focus on delightful details like hover effects, loading states, and smooth page transitions.

## Elixir guidelines

- Elixir lists do not support index access via the access syntax.

```elixir
# Never.
i = 0
mylist = ["blue", "green"]
mylist[i]

# Always.
i = 0
mylist = ["blue", "green"]
Enum.at(mylist, i)
```

- Elixir variables are immutable but can be rebound; for block expressions (`if`, `case`, `cond`), bind the result outside the expression.

```elixir
# Never.
if connected?(socket) do
  socket = assign(socket, :val, val)
end

# Always.
socket =
  if connected?(socket) do
    assign(socket, :val, val)
  end
```

- Never nest multiple modules in the same file.
- Never use map access (`changeset[:field]`) on structs; use direct field access or higher-level APIs like `Ecto.Changeset.get_field/2`.
- Use Elixir standard library for date/time; never add deps unless asked or for parsing (`date_time_parser` is allowed).
- Do not use `String.to_atom/1` on user input due to memory leak risk.
- Predicate function names should not start with `is_` and should end with a question mark; `is_` is reserved for guards.
- OTP primitives like `DynamicSupervisor` and `Registry` require names in the child spec, e.g. `{DynamicSupervisor, name: MyApp.MyDynamicSup}` and then `DynamicSupervisor.start_child(MyApp.MyDynamicSup, child_spec)`.
- Use `Task.async_stream(collection, callback, options)` for concurrent enumeration with back-pressure, and prefer `timeout: :infinity`.

## Mix guidelines

- Read docs and options before using tasks with `mix help task_name`.
- Debug test failures with `mix test test/my_test.exs` or run all previously failed tests with `mix test --failed`.
- Avoid `mix deps.clean --all` unless you have a good reason.

## Test guidelines

- Always use `start_supervised!/1` to start processes in tests to guarantee cleanup.
- Avoid `Process.sleep/1` and `Process.alive?/1` in tests.
- To wait for a process to finish, use `Process.monitor/1` and assert on the DOWN message:

```elixir
ref = Process.monitor(pid)
assert_receive {:DOWN, ^ref, :process, ^pid, :normal}
```

- To synchronize before the next call, use `_ = :sys.get_state/1` to ensure prior messages are handled.

## Phoenix guidelines

- Phoenix router `scope` blocks include an optional alias prefixed for all routes in the scope; be mindful to avoid duplicate module prefixes.
- You never need to create your own `alias` for route definitions because `scope` provides it.

```elixir
scope "/admin", AppWeb.Admin do
  pipe_through :browser

  live "/users", UserLive, :index
end
```

- `Phoenix.View` is no longer needed or included with Phoenix, so do not use it.

## Phoenix HTML guidelines

- Phoenix templates always use `~H` or `.html.heex` (HEEx), and never `~E`.
- Always use the imported `Phoenix.Component.form/1` and `Phoenix.Component.inputs_for/1` for forms, and never `Phoenix.HTML.form_for` or `Phoenix.HTML.inputs_for`.
- Build forms with `Phoenix.Component.to_form/2` and use `@form[:field]` in templates:

```heex
assign(socket, form: to_form(...))
<.form for={@form} id="msg-form">
```

- Always add unique DOM IDs to key elements (forms, buttons, etc.) so tests can reference them.
- For app-wide template imports, use `my_app_web.ex` in the `html_helpers` block so they are available to `use MyAppWeb, :html`.
- Elixir does not support `else if` or `elsif`; use `cond` or `case` for multiple conditionals.

```heex
<%= cond do %>
  <% condition -> %>
    ...
  <% condition2 -> %>
    ...
  <% true -> %>
    ...
<% end %>
```

- HEEx requires `phx-no-curly-interpolation` for literal `{` or `}` in `<pre>` or `<code>` blocks:

```heex
<code phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  let obj = {key: "val"}
</code>
```

- HEEx class attrs support lists and must use list syntax; wrap `if` in parens:

```heex
<a class={[
  "px-2 text-white",
  @some_flag && "py-5",
  if(@other_condition, do: "border-red-500", else: "border-blue-100")
]}>Text</a>
```

- Do not use invalid class attr syntax like this:

```heex
<a class={
  "px-2 text-white",
  @some_flag && "py-5"
}> ...
```

- Never use `<% Enum.each %>` or non-`for` comprehensions; always use `<%= for item <- @collection do %>`.
- HEEx HTML comments use `<%!-- comment --%>`, and you must use this syntax for template comments.
- `<%= %>` works only within tag bodies; use `{...}` for interpolation within tag attributes and for values within tag bodies, and use `<%= ... %>` for block constructs within tag bodies.

```heex
<div id={@id}>
  {@my_assign}
  <%= if @some_block_condition do %>
    {@another_assign}
  <% end %>
</div>
```

```heex
<%!-- INVALID --%>
<div id="<%= @invalid_interpolation %>">
  {if @invalid_block_construct do}
  {end}
</div>
```

## Phoenix LiveView guidelines

- Never use deprecated `live_redirect` or `live_patch`; use `<.link navigate={href}>` and `<.link patch={href}>` in templates, and `push_navigate` or `push_patch` in LiveViews.
- Avoid LiveComponents unless you have a strong, specific need.
- LiveViews should be named like `AppWeb.WeatherLive` with a `Live` suffix; the default `:browser` scope is already aliased with `AppWeb`, so use `live "/weather", WeatherLive`.

### LiveView streams

- Always use streams for collections to avoid memory ballooning, using:
  `stream(socket, :messages, [new_msg])`,
  `stream(socket, :messages, [new_msg], reset: true)`,
  `stream(socket, :messages, [new_msg], at: -1)`,
  `stream_delete(socket, :messages, msg)`.
- When using `stream/3`, the template must set `phx-update="stream"` on the parent with a DOM id and consume `@streams.stream_name` with the id as each child DOM id:

```heex
<div id="messages" phx-update="stream">
  <div :for={{id, msg} <- @streams.messages} id={id}>
    {msg.text}
  </div>
</div>
```

- Streams are not enumerable; to filter or refresh, refetch data and re-stream with `reset: true`:

```elixir
def handle_event("filter", %{"filter" => filter}, socket) do
  messages = list_messages(filter)

  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> assign(:messages_empty?, messages == [])
   |> stream(:messages, messages, reset: true)}
end
```

- Streams do not support counting or empty states; track counts in separate assigns. Use Tailwind for empty states and ensure the empty block is the only element alongside the `for` comprehension:

```heex
<div id="tasks" phx-update="stream">
  <div class="hidden only:block">No tasks yet</div>
  <div :for={{id, task} <- @stream.tasks} id={id}>
    {task.name}
  </div>
</div>
```

- When an assign change should affect streamed items, re-stream items with the updated assign:

```elixir
def handle_event("edit_message", %{"message_id" => message_id}, socket) do
  message = Chat.get_message!(message_id)
  edit_form = to_form(Chat.change_message(message, %{content: message.content}))

  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> stream_insert(:messages, message)
   |> assign(:editing_message_id, String.to_integer(message_id))
   |> assign(:edit_form, edit_form)}
end
```

```heex
<div id="messages" phx-update="stream">
  <div :for={{id, message} <- @streams.messages} id={id} class="flex group">
    {message.username}
    <%= if @editing_message_id == message.id do %>
      <%!-- Edit mode --%>
      <.form for={@edit_form} id="edit-form-#{message.id}" phx-submit="save_edit">
        ...
      </.form>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
```

- Never use deprecated `phx-update="append"` or `phx-update="prepend"` for collections.

### LiveView JavaScript interop

- When using `phx-hook="MyHook"` and the hook manages its own DOM, you must also set `phx-update="ignore"`.
- Always provide a unique DOM id with `phx-hook`, or a compiler error will be raised.
- LiveView hooks are either inline colocated hooks in HEEx or external hooks passed to `LiveSocket`.

#### Inline colocated JS hooks

- Never write raw embedded `<script>` tags in HEEx; use a colocated hook with `:type={Phoenix.LiveView.ColocatedHook}` when writing scripts inside templates:

```heex
<input type="text" name="user[phone_number]" id="user-phone-number" phx-hook=".PhoneNumber" />
<script :type={Phoenix.LiveView.ColocatedHook} name=".PhoneNumber">
  export default {
    mounted() {
      this.el.addEventListener("input", e => {
        let match = this.el.value.replace(/\D/g, "").match(/^(\d{3})(\d{3})(\d{4})$/)
        if(match) {
          this.el.value = `${match[1]}-${match[2]}-${match[3]}`
        }
      })
    }
  }
</script>
```

- Colocated hooks are automatically integrated into the `app.js` bundle.
- Colocated hook names must always start with a `.` prefix, e.g. `.PhoneNumber`.

#### External `phx-hook`

- External hooks (`<div id="myhook" phx-hook="MyHook">`) must be placed in `assets/js/` and passed to the `LiveSocket` constructor:

```javascript
const MyHook = {
  mounted() { ... }
}
let liveSocket = new LiveSocket("/live", Socket, {
  hooks: { MyHook }
});
```

#### Pushing events between client and server

- Use `push_event/3` to push events/data to the client, and always return or rebind the socket when pushing events:

```elixir
socket = push_event(socket, "my_event", %{...})

def handle_event("some_event", _, socket) do
  {:noreply, push_event(socket, "my_event", %{...})}
end
```

- Pushed events are handled in JS hooks with `this.handleEvent`:

```javascript
mounted() {
  this.handleEvent("my_event", data => console.log("from server:", data));
}
```

- Clients can push events to the server and receive replies with `this.pushEvent`:

```javascript
mounted() {
  this.el.addEventListener("click", e => {
    this.pushEvent("my_event", { one: 1 }, reply => console.log("got reply from server:", reply));
  })
}
```

```elixir
def handle_event("my_event", %{"one" => 1}, socket) do
  {:reply, %{two: 2}, socket}
end
```

### LiveView tests

- Use `Phoenix.LiveViewTest` and `LazyHTML` for assertions.
- Form tests are driven by `Phoenix.LiveViewTest.render_submit/2` and `Phoenix.LiveViewTest.render_change/2`.
- Create step-by-step test plans that split major cases into small, isolated files; start with simple content checks, then add interaction tests.
- Always reference key element IDs in LiveView tests with `element/2`, `has_element/2`, and selectors.
- Never test against raw HTML; use `element/2`, `has_element/2`, and similar, e.g. `assert has_element?(view, "#my-form")`.
- Favor presence of key elements over testing text content.
- `Phoenix.Component` functions like `<.form>` can produce different HTML; test against output HTML structure.
- When selectors fail, print actual HTML using LazyHTML selectors:

```elixir
html = render(view)
document = LazyHTML.from_fragment(html)
matches = LazyHTML.filter(document, "your-complex-selector")
IO.inspect(matches, label: "Matches")
```
